  <section id="verdict">
    <h2>The methods tested here do not show chambers; read from one shaking, the satellite needs {vibMillions} times the force geophones need</h2>
    <Boundary />
    <div class="stat-row spaced">
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{mph.toFixed(2)}<small>m/Hz</small></span><span class="what">of published “depth” per hertz of the registration’s oscillation, whatever lies below: the depth axis is a frequency axis, with a repeat and a mirror</span><span class="src">P2-06 · the published focusing, its algebra</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{pct(blk.gain_complex)}</span><span class="what">of a stretch of real ground’s planted motion recovered by correlating one image’s looks against each other, the published way</span><span class="src">P2-03 · measured on the real Giza image, for that estimator</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value"><Sci v={imp.cases[0].imprint_over_motion} /></span><span class="what">the fraction of the ground’s own motion a 6 m chamber 15 m down changes as a microseism passes, in uniform rock</span><span class="src">P2-04 · a quasi-static model</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{order(ground.shortfall!)}<small>×</small></span><span class="what">short, in our model: the smallest imprint one dwell could detect on the plateau against the imprint modelled ({order(bright.shortfall!)} on a bright scatterer)</span><span class="src">P2-05 · a model, every advantage we could name allowed</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="value">{sci(bd.static[0].ceiling)}</span><span class="what">the most any way of reading one image of ordinary ground can raise its detection rate above its false-alarm rate on that chamber under Giza’s regional microseism level ({sci(bSurf.truck.ceiling)} with a truck bouncing beside it all pass)</span><span class="src">P2-25 · a bound within the speckle model</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">
      The first is a property of the processing and holds whatever the ground does: the published depth coordinate is the
      frequency of each pixel’s registration series, and nothing yet supplied turns it into a physical depth; that would take a
      model linking a buried object to the surface’s motion and to the radar’s reading, tested on known voids. The second is
      measured, for complex correlation between one image’s <Term k="look">looks</Term> on real texture; bright points can be
      tracked, and any other estimator needs its own measurement. The third and fourth rest on models: a small chamber in
      uniform rock, shaken at Giza’s regional microseism level. Resonance, layering, larger structures or other forcing would
      change their numbers, though the gaps they find are wide. The fifth is of another kind: a bound on every way of reading
      one image of ordinary ground at once, tested or not, within a stated model (P2-25, <a href="#bound">below</a>); its
      inputs are those same models, extended by P2-26 to every frequency, and P2-27 turns it into the room-specific motion a
      detection would need. None of this shows that no radar could ever sense anything below the surface; it shows that these
      methods, as tested, do not, and that within the model no other way of reading one X-band image of ordinary ground
      could do much better. None of it has yet been checked against measured ground motion and radar data taken together. The measured record from the real acquisitions
      (<a href={`${base}/archive/`}>the first investigation</a>) agrees, and several of its readings, and of ours, are
      corrected below.
    </p>
    <p class="note credit">
      The stricter, gated reconstruction throughout is the public <a href={GATED_CODE.url}>{GATED_CODE.name}</a>
      ({GATED_CODE.version}, commit {GATED_CODE.commit}), run unchanged: its source and profile hash to
      {' '}<span class="mono">{GATED_CODE.module_sha256.slice(0, 12)}…</span> and <span class="mono">{GATED_CODE.profile_sha256.slice(0, 12)}…</span>,
      as every run’s record states.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bound">
    <h2>Within its model, one image of ordinary ground holds almost nothing of the chamber: under Giza’s regional microseism level no test’s detection rate can exceed its false-alarm rate by more than {sci(bd.static[0].ceiling)}</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Everything above tests methods by running them. This bounds them all at once, including methods not yet written,
      within a stated measurement model (P2-25). An image is made of the echo from what the radar wave reaches, the surface,
      and the surface’s motion during the pass; any method is a function of the image, so none can hold more about the
      chamber than the image does (the data-processing inequality). The echo is noted, not pursued: the bench chamber’s roof
      lies at least {Math.round(bd.penetration_db_x_band)} dB down at X band (P2-11). The surface is what any photograph
      shows. The motion is bounded here, generously: the method is told the ambient shaking exactly, the image has no
      receiver noise, and the rock no attenuation; each only raises the bound. The model: ordinary ground as fully developed
      speckle with no reference image, the chamber’s imprint as the lab’s elastic solver finds it, and the image formed by
      the Doppler-to-time relation (checked below against pulse-by-pulse physics).
    </p>
    <Routes />
    <p class="note">The theorems, the loose certificate that includes the ground’s background motion, and the oracle are set out together on <a href={`${base}/proof/`}>the proof page</a>; this section holds the tight calculation, its checks and every case.</p>
    <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 18px">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">a theorem within the model · fully developed speckle</span>
        <h3>The bound</h3>
        <p>For any test, detection rate − false-alarm rate ≤ TV ≤ min(√(KL/2), √(1 − e<sup>−KL</sup>)) (Pinsker,
        Bretagnolle–Huber); with the two cases equally likely, the best accuracy is ½ + TV/2. Ordinary ground is
        <Term k="speckle">speckle</Term>: the image is a zero-mean circular Gaussian vector, and the change from no chamber
        to the chamber is bounded exactly, not to first order: whitening the covariance change into <i>E</i>,
        KL ≤ ‖<i>E</i>‖²/(2(1 − ‖<i>E</i>‖)) with ‖<i>E</i>‖ ≤ √<i>F</i> + ε, <i>F</i> the
        <Term k="fisher">Fisher information</Term> and ε an explicit remainder (a power series in the imprint’s phase).
        With the spectrum’s bins independent</p>
        <p class="eq"><i>F</i> = Σ<sub><i>f</i>, <i>f</i>′</sub> |Φ̃(<i>f</i> − <i>f</i>′; <i>f</i>) − Φ̃(<i>f</i> − <i>f</i>′; <i>f</i>′)|²</p>
        <p>Φ̃(<i>k</i>; <i>f</i>) is the spatial Fourier coefficient at <i>k</i> of the imprint’s radar phase 4π<i>d</i>/λ at the slow
        time <i>t</i> = <i>f</i><sub>a</sub>/<i>K</i><sub>a</sub> at which the pass sees Doppler bin <i>f</i>, over the processed band.
        For a weak signal the best test reaches 2Φ(√<i>F</i>/2) − 1 ≈ 0.40√<i>F</i>; the bound allows 0.50√<i>F</i>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">three consequences</span>
        <h3>What survives of motion in speckle</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Motion shared by the scene holds nothing.</b> Φ̃ is then nonzero only at <i>k</i> = 0, where the difference vanishes: <i>F</i> = 0 exactly, not approximately.</li>
          <li><b>Any motion:</b> <i>F</i> ≤ 4<i>N</i>⟨Φ²⟩, <i>N</i> the independent resolution cells ({Math.round(bd.cells_per_m2)} per square metre here) and ⟨Φ²⟩ the imprint’s mean-square phase over them and over the pass.</li>
          <li><b>Slow, smooth motion:</b> <i>F</i> = (<i>R</i>/<i>V</i><sub>s</sub>)² Σ<sub>cells</sub> ⟨(∂<i>v</i>/∂<i>x</i>)²⟩. The texture is stretched along track by the line-of-sight velocity’s gradient times <i>R</i>/<i>V</i><sub>s</sub> ({bd.stretch_seconds.toFixed(1)} s at Giza), and only that stretch survives. Faster motion displaces faint copies of the texture by {bd.paired_echo_m_per_hz.toFixed(2)} m per hertz, and where they land on ground that moved differently they leave a trace too; the exact sum counts both.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="bound-h">Checked: the sums, the finite change, detection apart from estimation, and the model’s time relation</h3>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>check</th><th>result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The double sum against tr(C⁺C′C⁺C′) built scatterer by scatterer (a 32 × 8 image)</td><td>agree to <Sci v={Math.max(bd.checks.covariance.relative_difference, 1e-16)} p={0} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Motion shared by the whole scene</td><td><i>F</i> = {bd.checks.covariance.shared_motion_fisher}; the score’s value {sci(bd.checks.estimator.C_shared.largest_score)} (zero to rounding)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The exact form on the image’s own pixels (every case below) against the double sum</td><td>agree to <Sci v={Math.max(...bd.checks.grid.map((r) => r.relative_difference_mean), 1e-16)} p={0} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The finite change: exact KL (the covariance’s eigenvalues, a 128 × 16 image, peak phase to {klPhase.toFixed(1)} rad) against <i>F</i>/2 and the rigorous bound</td><td>exact KL up to {klExcess.toFixed(3)} × <i>F</i>/2; the bound {klHolds ? 'holds in every case' : 'fails in a case (see the run)'}</td></tr>
        {det.map((d, i) => (
          <tr><td>Detection, {d.realisations.toLocaleString('en-US')} images each way, deflection {d.deflection.toFixed(1)}: the exact likelihood-ratio test’s largest detection − false alarm, and the score test’s</td><td>{d.likelihood_ratio.largest_pd_minus_pfa.toFixed(3)} and {d.score.largest_pd_minus_pfa.toFixed(3)} (± {d.se.toFixed(3)}); weak-signal theory {d.weak_signal_best_test.toFixed(3)}; Pinsker from the exact KL {d.pinsker_from_exact_kl.toFixed(3)}, {detOver[i].toFixed(2)} times the likelihood-ratio test</td></tr>
        ))}
        <tr><td>Estimation, separately: the score on {bA.realisations} speckle images, a {bA.width_m} m imprint at {bA.f_hz} Hz boosted to {bA.peak_phase_rad.toFixed(2)} rad: its spread without, its shift with, against √<i>F</i> and <i>aF</i></td><td>{bA.spread_over_sqrtF.toFixed(2)} ± {bA.spread_se.toFixed(2)} and {bA.shift_over_aF.toFixed(2)} ± {bA.shift_se.toFixed(2)}; its estimate’s variance {bA.estimator_var_times_F.toFixed(2)} of the Cramér–Rao bound</td></tr>
