    <h3 class="bound-h">What one image would need: under the stated conditions, a detection needs the room itself to move the ground over it by at least {umText(rqAt(0.2, 8).D_necessary_m['0.9'])} at microseism frequencies, or {umText(rqAt(80, 8).D_necessary_m['0.9'])} at 80 Hz</h3>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="The least room-specific line-of-sight motion for a detection at 95% with 5% false alarms, against the footprint’s area"
        series={[
          ...rqMain.map((r, i) => ({ name: `${r.f_hz} Hz`, x: r.exact.map((e) => e.area_half_m2), y: r.exact.map((e) => e.D_necessary_m['0.9']), color: rqColours[i], width: 1.8 })),
          ...rqMain.map((r, i) => ({ name: `${r.f_hz} Hz, linearised`, x: r.linear.filter((e) => e.area_half_m2 >= r.exact[r.exact.length - 1].area_half_m2).map((e) => e.area_half_m2), y: r.linear.filter((e) => e.area_half_m2 >= r.exact[r.exact.length - 1].area_half_m2).map((e) => e.D_linearised_m['0.9']), color: rqColours[i], dash: '4 3', width: 1.2 })),
          { name: 'the bench room’s imprints (P2-04, P2-26)', x: rq.imprints.map((i) => i.area_half_m2), y: rq.imprints.map((i) => i.peak_m), color: 'var(--muted)', markers: true, width: 0 },
        ]}
        xLabel="footprint, m² within half the peak"
        yLabel="m, line of sight"
        logX
        logY
        yFormat="exp"
        height={250}
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`P2-27, on the Giza dwell of 27 August 2025. Solid: exact within the model (each line of the image’s KL computed from its covariance’s determinant, the range band widened, which can only add information); dashed: linearised, for footprints wider than the exact computation reaches. Points: the bench room’s modelled imprints, ${sci(rqStatic.short_by)} below the curve under the regional microseism level and ${sci(Math.min(...rqDyn.map((i) => i.short_by)))} to ${sci(Math.max(...rqDyn.map((i) => i.short_by)))} below it under a truck over a bump 15 m away.`}
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">conditional · P2-27</span>
        <h3>A requirement curve, not an exclusion of mechanisms</h3>
        <p>The bound turned around: how large must the motion the room itself adds to the ground be before any way of reading
        one image could find it? For the Giza dwell, ordinary ground as fully developed speckle with no reference image and no
        receiver noise, one room-specific motion pattern coherent for the whole pass at its worst phase, and a target of 95%
        found with 5% false alarms, at least {umText(rqAt(0.2, 8).D_necessary_m['0.9'])} of line-of-sight motion at 0.2 Hz
        (the stretch regime, where only its gradient counts and the footprint hardly matters) and
        {' '}{umText(rqAt(80, 8).D_necessary_m['0.9'])} at 80 Hz over {Math.round(rqAt(80, 8).area_half_m2)} m². The exact
        computation stays within {((rq.checks.largest_nonlinearity - 1) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of its quadratic start even at
        those amplitudes; the 2022 acquisition (look angle {rq.acquisitions['giza-20220715'].look_angle_deg.toFixed(0)}°) gives
        {' '}{umText(rqOld.find((r) => r.f_hz === 0.2)!.exact.find((e) => e.L_m === 8)!.D_necessary_m['0.9'])} and
        {' '}{umText(rqOld.find((r) => r.f_hz === 80)!.exact.find((e) => e.L_m === 8)!.D_necessary_m['0.9'])}.</p>
        <p>Any mechanism that acts through the surface’s motion must put such motion on the ground, whatever drives it, linear
        or not. A change of the ambient motion’s phase alone, of any size, moves the ground by at most twice that motion; P2-26’s
        rooms scatter at most {rq.constrained.modelled_ratio.toFixed(1)} times it. A mechanism limited so would need Giza’s regional
        levels raised at least {sci(rqLeast)} times ({sci(rqLeastAll)} for a lorry bouncing 15 m away all pass); one with gain,
        gathering energy over the room for the whole pass, would have to be specified before it could be computed. Outside the curve: changes
        of the surface’s reflectivity during the pass, the echo from beneath, scatterers that are not speckle, and more than
        one image. A corner reflector on the footprint’s peak needs {umText(rqMain[0].linear[0].D_reflector_m['0.9'])} whatever
        the footprint; a method handed the ground’s reflectivity, far less.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="bound-h">Told everything but the answer, an oracle still gains at most {sci(Math.max(...orAmb.map((r) => r.tv_exact)))} under the ambient levels; the open regime is strong shaking close by, known exactly</h3>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">exact · any texture · P2-29</span>
        <h3>The oracle</h3>
        <p>Give a detector more than any method has: the exact reflectivity of every scatterer, the background motion, the
        shaking’s realisation and both physical models, with the room and without. All it lacks is which is true, and the
        receiver noise. Its raw echoes under the two hypotheses are then Gaussian with the same covariance and means that differ
        by the echo of the room-specific motion alone, so its best test is known exactly, with no Pinsker step: detection −
        false alarm = 2Φ(Δ/2) − 1, Δ² = 2‖μ₁ − μ₀‖²/σ². A method told less does no better, whatever the ground’s texture:
        only independent, uniform phases are needed, and the background motion, however large, cancels exactly:
        E Δ² = 8 Σ<sub><i>j</i></sub> SNR<sub><i>j</i></sub> ⟨sin²(<i>k</i>₀δ<sub><i>j</i></sub>/2)⟩, δ the motion the room adds.
        Pulse by pulse on the real dwell ({orc.raw_echo_check.scatterers} scatterers, {orc.raw_echo_check.pulses.toLocaleString('en-US')} pulses) the formula
        holds to {orChk.background_x1.mean.toFixed(3)} ± {orChk.background_x1.se.toFixed(3)}, and to {orChk.background_x10.mean.toFixed(3)} ± {orChk.background_x10.se.toFixed(3)} with the
        background ten times larger. Each pixel is taken {orc.manifest.params.snr_db} dB over the receiver noise, above the
        brightest natural ground over ICEYE’s best Dwell noise floor ({orc.manifest.params.nesz_best_db} dB).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag">depth · any method</span>
        <h3>Depth needs far more than detection</h3>
        <p>Small rooms 15 m down, spread over the ground, leave the same mark as the bench room 30 m down to within
        {(100 * orc.depth.residual_fraction).toFixed(1)}% (the lab’s solver, each small room’s imprint added; two solved together
        agree with the sum to {(100 * orc.depth.pair_interaction_fraction).toFixed(1)}%). The bench room 15 m down alone matches it
        only to {Math.round(100 * orc.depth.best_single_bench_room_residual)}%. So even the oracle, told everything else, needs
        {Math.round(orc.depth.signal_factor_depth_over_detection)} times the signal to tell those two depths apart that it needs to
        detect either: over a class that admits spread-out voids, no method can recover depth reliably wherever it can barely
        detect. This is separate from the published depth axis being a frequency axis (P2-06, P2-28).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <table class="data compact">
      <thead><tr><th>the oracle, the bench room</th><th>Δ</th><th>detection − false alarm</th><th>motion must grow, for 95% at 5%</th><th>after allowances (×{Math.round(orc.allowance_product)})</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {orc.chamber.map((r) => (
          <tr><td>{r.case}</td><td>{sci(r.delta)}</td><td>{sci(r.tv_exact)}</td><td>{r.growth_to_target >= 100 ? sci(r.growth_to_target) : r.growth_to_target.toFixed(1)}×</td><td>{r.open ? 'open' : `${r.growth_after_allowances >= 100 ? sci(r.growth_after_allowances) : r.growth_after_allowances.toFixed(1)}×`}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      The allowances are assumptions, stated so the margin can be judged: the local ambient level ten times the regional,
      site amplification three times, and 10 dB more signal-to-noise. Where the margin does not survive them the case is
      open, and it is reported as open: strong shaking close to the room for the whole pass, with the oracle told that
      shaking exactly and the scattered wave carried unattenuated across the whole scene. That regime needs a physical limit on
      how much motion a room can add, which the statistics cannot supply.
    </p>

    <h3 class="bound-h">{sharpAny || ringQ > 20 || ringUnresolved.length ? `Faster shaking reaches a room better, and ${ringQ > 20 ? `a room rung on its ceiling holds a mode with Q ${ringQ.toFixed(0)}` : 'some responses peak sharply'}: the bounds use the imprint as the solver finds it` : `In the rooms modelled the responses are broad: rung on its ceiling, the longest-lived mode resolved has Q ${ringQ.toFixed(0)}; a room under a 5 m roof moves the ground over it up to ${spF.peak_value.toFixed(1)} times the passing motion`}</h3>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        title="The chamber’s imprint at every frequency: its largest line-of-sight value over the incident motion"
        series={[
          ...spNames.filter(([k]) => sp.cases[k]).map(([k, name, color]) => ({ name, x: sp.cases[k].f_hz, y: sp.cases[k].peak_transfer, color, width: k === 'surface' ? 1.9 : 1.3 })),
          { name: 'static answer (P2-04)', x: fStatic, y: fStatic.map(staticLine), color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '4 3', width: 1.2 },
        ]}
        xLabel="Hz"
        yLabel="imprint / motion"
        logY
        height={210}
        directLabels={false}
        caption={`The lab’s elastic solver, with and without the chamber, waves from the side and from below, in rock with no attenuation, records of 0.3–0.5 s (P2-26). The largest imprint under a surface wave is ${spS.peak_value.toFixed(2)} of the motion at ${Math.round(spS.peak_f_hz)} Hz (${widthText(spS)}: the width of the largest response’s envelope, not a modal Q); ${sharpAny ? 'peaks standing 20% above their surroundings are listed in the run' : 'no curve has a peak standing 20% above its surroundings at these records’ resolution'}. Halving the grid changes the imprint by at most ${Math.round(100 * sp.grid_check.surface_small.max_amplitude_change)}% below 120 Hz (${Math.round(100 * sp.grid_check.surface_favourable_small.max_amplitude_change)}% for the room under a 5 m roof).`}
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P2-26</span>
        <h3>These rooms, excitations and windows gave broad responses</h3>
        <p>A resonance is the one way physics could make the imprint far larger than the static answer, and a short record
        cannot exclude a long-lived narrow one (a 60 Hz mode with Q 600 takes 3 s to fall by 1/e). So each room was also struck
        on its ceiling and recorded for {ringLen} s beside intact rock: the largest modal Q resolved from the spectrum’s peaks
        at {ringRes.toFixed(2)} Hz is {ringQ.toFixed(1)}, the energy falls 60 dB within {ringT60.toFixed(2)} s (intact rock:
        {' '}{ringCtlT60.toFixed(2)} s){ringUnresolved.length ? `, and peaks narrower than the resolution appear at ${ringUnresolved.slice(0, 4).map((f) => f.toFixed(1)).join(', ')} Hz` : ''}.
        Over {longLen} s of a passing surface wave, {(100 * lateShare).toFixed(2)}% of the imprint’s energy at the ground comes
        after the wave has passed. Under the surface wave the bench room’s imprint grows with frequency until the wave is short
        enough to pass over the room; the room under a 5 m roof flexes with it, up to {spF.peak_value.toFixed(1)} times the
        passing motion near {Math.round(spF.peak_f_hz)} Hz ({spFfine.peak_value.toFixed(1)} near {Math.round(spFfine.peak_f_hz)} Hz on
        the finer grid). At the low end the imprint meets P2-04’s static answer ({dyn7[0].toFixed(2)} of it at
        {' '}{Math.round(sp.static.f_hz[0])} Hz). Only these rooms, excitations and windows are covered: fractured, layered or coupled
        structures, and the air inside a room, are not modelled.</p>
        <p>What the faster shaking lacks is strength and duration. The bound counts the imprint within 32 m exactly and the
        wave it scatters out across the whole {(2 * bd.scene_radius_m / 1000).toFixed(0)} km scene by the bound 4<i>N</i>⟨Φ²⟩,
        unattenuated; within 32 m alone the urban hum would give {sci(bSurf.urban.ceiling_near_only)} and the truck
        {' '}{sci(bSurf.truck.ceiling_near_only)}.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">
      What the bound assumes: ordinary ground is fully developed speckle (bright points are bounded separately, above, and
      the genie row holds for any scene); the image forms by the Doppler-to-time relation; the rock is modelled as linearly
      elastic and unattenuated (its nonlinear response is not modelled); the chamber is the bench’s, a 6 m room 15 m down,
      shallower and more favourable than anything claimed; the ambient levels are the regional and published ones cited.
      What it does not cover: penetration at longer wavelengths (P band could reach a shallow room, blurred into a 25 m
      cell), changes of the surface’s reflectivity during the pass, texture between speckle and bright points, and many
      images, which can measure slow deformation (P2-11). No part of it has yet been checked against measured ground motion
      and radar data taken together: the geophone-and-satellite comparison (P2-22, P2-23) is simulated.
    </p>
  </section>

