  <section id="questions">
    <h2>A satellite can measure a bright target shaking; in our tests ordinary ground’s trembling barely registers, and perfect measurements read the published way would not locate a chamber</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">measured · 2026 papers, P2-03</span>
        <h3>Can a satellite measure vibration from one pass?</h3>
        <p>Yes, for bright, isolated targets that move a lot: in 2026, corner reflectors on shakers and a steel footbridge
        were measured from orbit at {(eB.smallest_measured_m_s * 1e3).toFixed(2)} mm/s and up, to about 1 mm/s. Ordinary ground is
        much harder. In our test on the real Giza image, correlating one image’s looks recovered {pct(blk.gain_complex)} of a patch
        of ground’s planted motion, and Giza’s natural trembling is {sig2(eB.measured_over_trembling)} times below the smallest
        motion measured from orbit so far. The 77 µm/s once quoted here was the scatter of a reading that barely responds to the
        ground’s motion, not a sensitivity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P2-06, P1-04</span>
        <h3>If every pixel’s vibration were known perfectly, would it show a chamber?</h3>
        <p>Not at the right depth by the published method: its depth is the frequency of the motion, so given the exact motion
        at every pixel it puts the trembling at one depth over rock and over a chamber alike; it would still draw it brighter
        where the chamber changes the motion, so where the chamber is could survive with its depth wrong (P2-06). Nor, in our simulations, by passive seismic imaging within one pass: the ground’s
        trembling comes in waves kilometres long, too coarse to outline a room, and a pass lasts {gz.aperture_s.toFixed(1)} s.
        Even with short waves, perfect instruments on the ground and the rock known exactly, passive imaging shows the chamber
        no brighter than its surroundings after a minute of recording
        ({(passive.score.contrast_vs_record as { record_s: number; contrast: number }[]).find((r) => r.record_s === 60)!.contrast.toFixed(1)}×),{' '}
        {(passive.score.contrast_vs_record as { record_s: number; contrast: number }[]).find((r) => r.record_s === 3600)!.contrast.toFixed(1)}× after
        an hour, and never 3×.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P2-09, P2-07</span>
        <h3>Isn’t a million virtual seismometers better than a few real ones?</h3>
        <p>Only if they hear. In our test, made into {vsens.sensors.n.toLocaleString('en-US')} virtual sensors and shaken by a wave
        put into the image, they recovered {pct(vsens.gains.complex)} of it read the published way. Averaging deaf sensors gives a precise zero.
        Where magnitudes hear a fifth, the chamber would need <Sci v={vsens.count.needed_for_chamber} p={0} /> sensors over it; the
        whole image holds <Sci v={vsens.count.in_image} p={0} />. Over the chamber, one pass’s reading wanders
        {' '}{sig2(ccmp.satellite_rms_m_s / ccmp.seismometer_rms_m_s)} times more than a real seismometer
        records there.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P1-02 to P1-05, P2-03</span>
        <h3>Seismic instruments find the chamber; why doesn’t the satellite, here?</h3>
        <p>The ones that find it make their own waves. A hammer blow sends a sharp wave about {Math.round((imp.manifest.params.vs as number) / (echo.manifest.params.f0_hz as number))} m long,
        timed exactly, and instruments pressed against the ground record its echo within {Math.round((echo.manifest.params.record_s as number) * 1000)} ms: the
        chamber comes back {echo.score.contrast.toFixed(0)}× brighter than its surroundings. Listening to the ground’s own trembling instead works
        only weakly, even with perfect instruments ({passive.score.image_contrast_ensemble.toFixed(1)}× with unlimited listening). A satellite has no hammer,
        listens for {gz.aperture_s.toFixed(1)} s, registers little of ordinary ground’s trembling in our tests, and its looks follow slow
        motion best, a few tenths of a hertz, whose waves are kilometres long.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="benchmark">
    <h2>The benchmark has begun: the simulator holds up against pulses, a vibration a tracker measures leaves the gated pipeline silent, and {bFirst === undefined ? 'over a known chamber its picture does not depend on the chamber' : 'over a known chamber its picture responds only when the chamber is boosted'}</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated pulse by pulse · P2-18</span>
        <h3>The radar simulator, checked against pulses</h3>
        <p>A second simulator computes what the radar records pulse by pulse, from every scatterer’s exact distance at each
        moment, displaced by its own motion, and forms the image by back-projection on the product’s grid. It focuses a point
        to {(eRes.azimuth_m * 100).toFixed(2)} cm in azimuth against {(eRes.azimuth_theory_m * 100).toFixed(2)} cm in theory. Its frequency
        slices match the images formed from only the matching stretches of pulses, {Math.min(...eSame).toFixed(3)} to
        {' '}{Math.max(...eSame).toFixed(3)} in complex correlation, with the mirrored slices at {eMirror.toFixed(3)}: a Doppler slice is a
        stretch of the pass, shown by a calculation that never assumes it. The fast synthesizer’s image of the same scatterers
        matches it to {(pulses.fast_vs_pulses as { corr_complex: number }).corr_complex.toFixed(2)}, and a steady
        {' '}{((pulses.steady_velocity as { v_m_s: number }).v_m_s * 1e3).toFixed(0)} mm/s recession shifts the image
        {' '}{Math.abs((pulses.steady_velocity as { shift_m: number }).shift_m * 100).toFixed(1)} cm against
        {' '}{Math.abs((pulses.steady_velocity as { theory_m: number }).theory_m * 100).toFixed(1)} cm from theory. Through the six-second
        pairs, pulses and the fast injection agree where the shifts are resolvable
        ({eBig.map((x) => `${x.pulses_px.toFixed(3)} and ${x.injection_px.toFixed(3)} px at ${(x.v_m_s * 1e3).toFixed(0)} mm/s, ${x.f_hz.toFixed(2)} Hz`).join('; ')}),
        and the rest sit at or below one rounding step in both.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
