  <section id="settle">
    <h2>What would settle it: one frozen recipe reproduced, a motion test with ground truth, and a blind test on known voids</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="note-card"><h3>Reproduce one nominated result</h3><p>Take one figure the method’s proponents consider successful, with its image, exact geometry, code version, settings and plotting frozen and hashed, and have an independent reviewer regenerate it. Then run that frozen recipe on motionless copies, open ground and a planted motion. So far, attractive figures and negative tests have concerned different recipes.</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><h3>Show that the estimator measures motion</h3><p>Run the frozen pipeline on stationary scenes, on moving targets simulated from independent physics, and preferably on shaker or corner-reflector data with instruments on them at the same time. Sweep frequency and amplitude, 0.26 and 3.66 Hz included, and score recovery before the gates as well as after, beside an estimator already validated for vibrometry, so that a faulty injection is not mistaken for a failed method. Begun below with synthetic products (P2-19).</p></div>
      <div class="note-card"><h3>Predict known voids blind</h3><p>Choose known voids and matched surveyed controls and withhold their labels. Fix the wavelength, line placement, gates and scoring in advance; predict places, depths and sizes with tolerances; count false positives as well as hits; use a site or pass not used for calibration. Before reading stairs or spirals, a forward model built on independent wave and radar physics should tell a plain column, a spiral and nothing apart.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      What would change this page: a frozen recipe that passes these tests, or measurements that contradict our models, such as
      motion measured on the plateau during a pass, penetration measured in Mokattam limestone, or a pulse-level simulation of
      the pairs that registers more than ours. A bounded validation of this kind is worth more than further compelling
      pictures, whichever way it comes out.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="fold-note mono">The evidence in detail · each section opens on its heading</p>

  <section id="geometry" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>The real dwell has three speeds, and the simulator now has all three</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      A <Term k="dwell">Spotlight Dwell</Term> holds the beam on Giza for {gz.aperture_s.toFixed(1)} s. The platform moves at
      {' '}{kms(gz.speeds_m_s.platform)} m/s, the image’s rows sweep the ground at {kms(gz.speeds_m_s.ground_sweep)} m/s,
      and the Doppler history runs at {kms(gz.speeds_m_s.effective)} m/s, their geometric mean to
      {' '}{Math.abs((gz.veff2_over_vs_vg - 1) * 1e6).toFixed(0)} parts per million. The first simulator used one speed and put the
      Doppler rate {Math.abs(gz.flat_earth_rate_error.with_ground_speed * 100).toFixed(0)}% off. Every second of aperture is
      also a change of viewing direction, and a look’s resolution times its duration is fixed by the orbit: a
      {' '}{gz.looks[3].duration_s} s look resolves {gz.looks[3].resolution_m.toFixed(2)} m, a 0.25 s look {gz.looks[0].resolution_m.toFixed(1)} m.
      Looks are cut from the image’s Doppler band, and for each pixel every Doppler frequency is a moment of the pass,
      counted from that pixel’s own zero-Doppler moment: its Doppler falls by
      {' '}{Math.abs(gz.doppler_rate_hz_s).toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 })} Hz every second, so slicing the band in
      frequency is slicing each pixel’s {gz.aperture_s.toFixed(1)} s in time, and a slice half the band wide averages
      {' '}{(gz.aperture_s / 2).toFixed(1)} s of whatever the ground did. Across a patch the size of a pyramid those zero-Doppler
      moments differ by hundredths of a second.
    </p>
    <DwellDiagram />
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Giza</th><th>Sacsayhuamán</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{acquisitions.map((r) => <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td class="num">{r[1]}</td><td class="num">{r[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
