  <section id="imprint" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>In our model, a chamber changes the ground’s own motion by less than a ten-thousandth of it</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      Whatever a satellite could use to find a chamber passively would have to be its <Term k="imprint">imprint</Term>: the
      difference it makes to how the surface moves. Ambient waves are far longer than the chamber and its depth, so the rock
      around it is strained almost uniformly and almost statically. The lab’s solver answers the static question directly:
      load the rock with a uniform horizontal stress, open the chamber, and let the ground settle. It is a model: one small
      chamber in uniform rock, strained quasi-statically; layering, the sand cover, resonance and other forcing are not in it.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        title="Fig. S4 · The surface over a 6 m chamber 15 m down, in rock under 1 MPa of horizontal tension"
        series={[
          { name: 'the lab’s solver', x: prof.x_m as number[], y: (prof.solver_uz_m as number[]).map(um), color: 'var(--verdigris)', width: 2 },
          { name: 'Eshelby’s void, Okada’s point sources', x: prof.x_m as number[], y: (prof.analytic_uz_m as number[]).map(um), color: 'var(--lapis)', dash: '5 3' },
        ]}
        xLabel="distance from above the chamber, m"
        yLabel="vertical displacement, µm"
        zeroLine
        caption={`Without the chamber nothing moves. On a 0.5 m grid the epicentre sinks ${Math.abs(um(conv[2].epicentre_uz_m)).toFixed(1)} µm against ${Math.abs(um(conv[0].epicentre_uz_m)).toFixed(1)} µm on 1 m; domains from 100 to 180 m wide agree to four digits. The analytic curve is a point sphere of equal volume.`}
      />
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P2-04</span>
        <h3>The imprint on the line-of-sight velocity</h3>
        <table class="data compact">
          <thead><tr><th>the ground’s motion</th><th>moves at</th><th>imprint</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {cases.map((c) => <tr><td>{c.case}</td><td class="num"><Sci v={um(c.vertical_velocity_m_s)} /> µm/s</td><td class="num"><Sci v={um(c.imprint_los_velocity_m_s)} /> µm/s</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          A passing <Term k="rayleigh">Rayleigh wave</Term> strains the ground by its horizontal-to-vertical ratio times its
          speed of motion over its phase speed, whatever its frequency, so the imprint is a fixed fraction of the motion for a
          given wave speed: <Sci v={imp.cases[0].imprint_over_motion} /> for microseisms. The truck is only a guide: at 15 Hz the
          waves are 110 m long, not long compared with the depth.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={imp.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="budget" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>Within our model, allowing every advantage we could name, one dwell falls short by ten thousand to a hundred million</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      The smallest imprint a dwell could detect, from what P2-03 measured: every one of its independent looks counted as a
      full measurement, a 3-sigma threshold, the look’s gain at the imprint’s frequency (a boxcar value; near 0.2 Hz it is
      within about 15% of the tracker’s measured gain), and the best tracker for each target. Under Giza’s microseisms that is {um(ground.a_min_m_s!).toFixed(0)} µm/s on the plateau’s own texture,
      {' '}{um(bright.a_min_m_s!).toFixed(0)} µm/s on a bright scatterer directly above the chamber, and {um(refl.a_min_m_s!).toFixed(0)} µm/s
      on a corner reflector that no archive image contains, against an imprint of <Sci v={um(ground.imprint_m_s)} /> µm/s. For the
      plateau the ground would have to shake at {ground.shake_needed_m_s!.toFixed(0)} m/s, beyond the strongest earthquakes
      recorded, for the whole dwell.
    </p>
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>short by</th>{caseNames.map((c) => <th>{c}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {targets.map((t) => (
          <tr><td>{t}</td>{caseNames.map((c) => { const r = cell(c, t); return <td class="num">{r.shortfall === null ? 'no response' : order(r.shortfall)}</td>; })}</tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      The threshold is checked by simulation: without motion the bright scatterer’s fitted sway scatters by
      {' '}{um(chk.null_rms_m_s).toFixed(0)} µm/s against the {um(chk.predicted_sigma_m_s).toFixed(0)} µm/s the formula uses, and a sway at
      the threshold is found in {pct(chk.detected_fraction)} of {chk.null_fits_m_s.length} realisations at a 5% false-alarm rate.
      Motion at 1–3 Hz needs 250 ms looks, resolving 4 m; the truck needs 33 ms looks, resolving 33 m, during its few seconds.
      These are estimates, not measurements of Giza during a pass: the ambient level is the regional microseism level
      recorded 67 km away, and the imprint is P2-04’s model.
    </p>
    <RunDetails manifest={bud.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="depth" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>The published depth is the frequency of the registration series: {mph.toFixed(2)} m per hertz, with a repeat and a mirror</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      The published focusing projects each patch’s trajectory on a steering wave whose wavenumber grows with the platform’s
      offset, and on a dwell the offset grows steadily with time. So the focusing is a Fourier transform over time, and motion
      at frequency f appears at a depth of f times λ<sub>s</sub> R sin θ / 2V<sub>s</sub>, whatever is under the ground; with
      regular sampling each depth also has a repeat and a mirror. This is a property of the processing, not of Giza.
      Frequency-dependent imaging is legitimate geophysics; what is missing is a physical model showing why this relation
      describes a buried object, and validation on known voids. The scale depends on λ<sub>s</sub>, a free parameter: this
      page uses the paper’s 0.48 m, and an independent review notes that the paper is inconsistent on it, its text implying
      0.48 m and a displayed expression 0.24 m, the value the reconstruction uses. With 0.24 m every depth halves and nothing
      else changes. Given the exact motion at every pair, the focusing draws the same peak over two chambers and over open
      ground (Fig. S6), differing only in brightness.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      What this shows is that depth is not identified: it is uncalibrated, it repeats and mirrors, and changing λ<sub>s</sub>
      rescales it without changing the fit. It does not show that a recovered horizontal position or outline is an artefact:
      brightness still changes where the motion changes, and a method that drew the right layout at an aliased depth would
      be useful. Horizontal location, shape, relative vertical geometry and absolute depth are scored as separate claims
      (<a href={`${base}/proof/#claims`}>the proof page</a>); run whole with the chamber’s imprint boosted, the published
      picture changes {(chainSweepRatio).toFixed(1)} times more over the chamber than elsewhere (P2-07), a paired comparison
      the method never has.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        title="Fig. S5 · Where the published focusing puts motion of each frequency"
        series={[
          { name: 'f λs R sin θ / 2Vs, folded', x: fline, y: fline.map(fold), color: 'var(--muted)', width: 1.4 },
          { name: 'the pipeline, given the exact motion', x: drow.map((r) => r.f_hz), y: drow.map((r) => r.depth_m), color: 'var(--porphyry)', markers: true },
        ]}
        xLabel="frequency of the ground's motion, Hz"
        yLabel="published depth, m"
        caption={`The 2022 paper’s bank on the real geometry samples time at ${fsamp.toFixed(1)} Hz, so its whole axis, 0 to ${(dep.nyquist_depth_m as number).toFixed(1)} m, is 0 to ${(fsamp / 2).toFixed(1)} Hz, and faster motion folds back: Giza’s ${tones.giza_ground.f_hz} Hz ground resonance would be drawn at ${tones.giza_ground.depth_m.toFixed(1)} m, Khufu’s ${tones.khufu.f_hz} Hz at ${tones.khufu.depth_m.toFixed(1)} m.`}
      />
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        title="Fig. S6 · The same microseism over two chambers and over open ground"
        series={[
          { name: 'chamber 15 m down', x: zax, y: prof6['chamber 15 m down'].tomogram, color: 'var(--lapis)', width: 3 },
          { name: 'chamber 30 m down', x: zax, y: prof6['chamber 30 m down'].tomogram, color: 'var(--porphyry)', dash: '6 4', width: 2 },
          { name: 'open ground', x: zax, y: prof6['open ground, the wave itself'].tomogram, color: 'var(--text)', dash: '1 3', width: 1.5 },
        ]}
        xLabel="published depth, m"
        yLabel="focused power, normalised"
        caption={`All three peak at ${prof6['chamber 15 m down'].depth_m.toFixed(2)} m, the depth of 0.2 Hz, and agree to rounding error; only their brightness differs.`}
      />
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={dep.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="chain" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>Run whole on our simulated ground, the chain does not tell the chamber is there</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      P2-07 runs the claim as it would have it: simulated desert over the one-chamber bench, shaking at Giza’s measured
      microseism level with the chamber’s imprint from P2-04, imaged on the real dwell in double precision, and put through
      the published pipeline unchanged (50 half-band pairs 88 Hz apart, 32-pixel patches, λ<sub>s</sub> = 0.48 m). The
      chamber changes the image by <Sci v={chain.A_vs_B.image_relative_difference} p={0} /> of itself (its imprint is{' '}
      <Sci v={chain.A_vs_B.imprint_phase_rad} p={0} /> rad of phase) and the depth section by{' '}
      <Sci v={chain.A_vs_B.method_relative_difference} p={0} />. The dashed outline is the chamber; with the published
      λ<sub>s</sub> the axis ends at {czmax.toFixed(1)} m, and any other λ<sub>s</sub> rescales depth without changing the fit (P2-06): a calibration ambiguity.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-4">
      <Figure compact src={cfig('method_A.png')} alt="Depth section with the chamber" label="With the chamber" caption="" aspect="4 / 5" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${czmax.toFixed(1)} m`}><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"><rect x={croom.x} y={croom.y} width={croom.w} height={102 - croom.y} fill="none" stroke="#82b3ff" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="1.6 1.1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></Figure>
      <Figure compact src={cfig('method_B.png')} alt="Depth section without the chamber" label="Without" caption="" aspect="4 / 5" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${czmax.toFixed(1)} m`}><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"><rect x={croom.x} y={croom.y} width={croom.w} height={102 - croom.y} fill="none" stroke="#82b3ff" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="1.6 1.1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></Figure>
      <Figure compact src={cfig('method_difference.png')} alt="Their difference" label="Difference" caption="" aspect="4 / 5" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${czmax.toFixed(1)} m`}><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"><rect x={croom.x} y={croom.y} width={croom.w} height={102 - croom.y} fill="none" stroke="#82b3ff" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="1.6 1.1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></Figure>
      <Figure compact src={cfig('method_boosted.png')} alt="Depth section with the imprint boosted" label={`Imprint × 10${String(Math.round(Math.log10(chain.shown_gain as number))).replace(/\d/g, (d) => '⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹'[+d])}`} caption="" aspect="4 / 5" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${czmax.toFixed(1)} m`}><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none"><rect x={croom.x} y={croom.y} width={croom.w} height={102 - croom.y} fill="none" stroke="#82b3ff" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="1.6 1.1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></Figure>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">simulated · P2-07</span>
        <h3>Boosting the imprint until the method responds</h3>
        <table class="data compact">
          <thead><tr><th>imprint ×</th><th>phase, rad</th><th>section change</th><th>over the chamber</th><th>elsewhere</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {sweep.map((r) => <tr><td class="num"><Sci v={r.gain} p={0} /></td><td class="num"><Sci v={r.imprint_phase_rad} /></td><td class="num">{pct(r.method_relative_difference)}</td><td class="num">{pct(r.over_chamber)}</td><td class="num">{pct(r.elsewhere)}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Visible change needs the imprint multiplied by <Sci v={chain.shown_gain as number} p={0} />, tens of radians of phase, and even then it is spread over every depth ({(chain.boosted_change_spread as number[])[0].toFixed(2)}–{(chain.boosted_change_spread as number[])[1].toFixed(2)} of its mean): the texture is reshuffled, nothing is drawn at the chamber.</p>
      </div>
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        title="Fig. S7 · Over the chamber: a seismometer against the dwell’s velocity series (R4’s reading)"
        series={[
          { name: 'seismometer', x: ccmp.seismometer_t_s as number[], y: (ccmp.seismometer_m_s as number[]).map(um), color: 'var(--ochre)', width: 2 },
          { name: 'the dwell', x: ccmp.look_times_s as number[], y: (ccmp.satellite_m_s as number[]).map(um), color: 'var(--cinnabar)', markers: true },
        ]}
        xLabel="s"
        yLabel="line-of-sight velocity, µm/s"
        zeroLine
        caption={`The ground moves ${um(ccmp.seismometer_rms_m_s).toFixed(3)} µm/s; the series wanders ${Math.round(um(ccmp.satellite_rms_m_s))} µm/s, ${Math.round(ccmp.satellite_rms_m_s / ccmp.seismometer_rms_m_s).toLocaleString('en-US')} times more, and not with it.`}
      />
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={chain.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="pillars" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>The published pictures’ pillars and boxes are the method’s own arithmetic</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      The published Khafre renderings show tall columns, banded like spirals, with blocks at their feet. P2-08 draws the same
      shapes from the real Khafre image with the published focusing, and each has a plain cause. <b>Pillars:</b> averaged over
      a period of its depth axis, the power the method draws at a pixel is that pixel’s trajectory energy (correlation{' '}
      {pk.pillar_corr_mean_power_vs_energy.toFixed(3)}), so a pixel whose registration wanders is bright at every depth and a
      thick-slab rendering stands it up as a column. Such pixels lie where the surface is darker or changes brightness; open
      plateau with no monument draws them too, and uniform simulated ground draws none. <b>Bands:</b> along a
      column the power rises and falls every {pk.resolution_m_shown.toFixed(0)} m of the relabelled axis, its resolution.{' '}
      <b>Blocks:</b> at the surface and at each repeat depth, {rep1.toLocaleString('en-US')} m and {(2 * rep1).toLocaleString('en-US')} m relabelled,
      every steering phase is the same, so the power there is the pixel’s average offset (ratio{' '}
      {pk.box_power_over_mean_offset.toFixed(3)}); below {rep1.toFixed(0)} m the picture repeats.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <Figure compact src={pfig('khafre_section.png')} alt="The published method's depth section at Khafre" label="Khafre, the real image" caption="columns where the registration wanders" aspect="5 / 7" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${pk.depth_shown_m.toFixed(0)} m`} />
      <Figure compact src={pfig('plateau_section.png')} alt="The same section over open plateau" label="Open plateau, no monument" caption="columns here too, with no monument" aspect="5 / 7" yTop="0 m" yBottom={`${pk.depth_shown_m.toFixed(0)} m`} />
      <Figure compact src={pfig('simulated_section.png')} alt="The same section over uniform simulated ground" label="Uniform simulated ground" caption="no columns: nothing wanders more than anything else" aspect="5 / 7" yTop="0 m" yBottom="" />
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Rendered as the published pictures are: a thick slab, a log scale and a rainbow palette. The claimed chambers near
      1,220 m sit close to the second repeat, {(2 * rep1).toLocaleString('en-US')} m relabelled; that is suggestive only, since the
      published relabelling is not known exactly. A published slice along a line is a row of pixels, each focused from its
      own shifts alone, so a volume is every row. The method’s “inversion” is only a Fourier transform of each pixel’s own shifts,
      with no waves modelled through the ground: for the Khafre patch’s
      {(pk.grid[0] * pk.grid[1]).toLocaleString('en-US')} pixels it takes a twentieth of a second, where a real seismic inversion of one
      small block takes hours (full-waveform inversion, on the seismic page). The costly step is measuring the shifts, which
      grows with the number of pixels; the lab refines each correlation peak locally (Guizar-Sicairos and others, 2008) instead of
      upsampling whole patches, so that patch took minutes, where every pixel of the whole image would take about two months.
    </p>
    <RunDetails manifest={pill.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="gates" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>Stricter gates select a pattern that also appears without motion; by themselves they certify neither vibration nor depth</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      Later versions of the method put selection gates between the shifts and the depth focus. Pairs of images are cut from
      the Doppler band, each {Math.round(gates.manifest.params.bank.mask_width_hz).toLocaleString('en-US')} Hz of it, which for every
      pixel is {gSpan.toFixed(1)} s of its pass, and registered to 0.01 px. A window of {gW} successive pairs is kept only if
      the two components of its shifts trace an ellipse at one of ten modes, fitted well, with a narrow semi-axis of at least
      {' '}{gg.minor_min_px} px and at least a tenth as wide as it is long; ten windows in a row must agree on the mode, and four
      or five neighbouring positions must pass together. The depth focus runs only where all of that passed. We ran such a
      gated pipeline, the public <a href={GATED_CODE.url}>{GATED_CODE.name}</a> ({GATED_CODE.version}), unchanged, twice. The first run (P2-13) used loops the lab drew up Khafre’s faces, from
      {' '}{gates.heights_m[0]} to {gates.heights_m[gates.heights_m.length - 1]} m, because we misread the reconstruction’s
      “70.5 m” as a height above the base; its own lines follow the base, 70.5 m above sea level, and the second run (P2-14,
      below) uses them. Both run the real 2022 image beside two motionless twins of it (its brightness and spectrum, fresh
      speckle), the same lines over open desert in the same image, and the real image with two stretches made to vibrate at
      2 mm/s along the line of sight, some 40,000 times Giza’s microseisms, at 0.26 Hz and 3.66 Hz, resonances proposed for a
      648 m column of air and for the Grand Gallery.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">real image · P2-13 · the lab’s loops on the faces</span>
        <h3>Positions that pass every gate</h3>
        <table class="data compact">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">positions</th><th class="num">features</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>the real image</td><td class="num">{gc.real}</td><td class="num">{gRuns.real}</td></tr>
            {gc.twins.map((n, i) => <tr><td>a motionless twin{i ? ', other speckle' : ''}</td><td class="num">{n}</td><td class="num">{gRuns.twins[i]}</td></tr>)}
            <tr><td>open desert, the same image</td><td class="num">{gc.desert}</td><td class="num">{gRuns.desert}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>the real image, vibrations planted</td><td class="num">{gc.planted}</td><td class="num">{gRuns.planted}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>{gatesVerdict}</p>
        <p>At the first gate the order is reversed: the real image passes {(gFirst.real / gFirstTwin).toFixed(1)} times as many
        windows as the twins, and over open desert {(gFirst.desert / gFirstTwin).toFixed(1)} times; its shifts are a little less
        steady ({pct(gZero)} exactly zero, against {pct(gZeroTwin)}). The twins and the desert are stress tests, not a calibrated
        false-alarm rate: they show the selected pattern can arise with nothing moving and nothing inside, under that
        generator, but two synthetic images cannot say how often it arises over real ground, and open desert is not surveyed
        ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag simulated">measured in simulation · P2-15</span>
        <h3>What the pairs register of a vibration</h3>
        <p>The two images of a pair share {(gates.pair_shared_fraction * 100).toFixed(2)}% of their spectrum; in the real image
        {' '}{pct(gZero)} of the shifts are exactly zero and {pct(gStep)} within one 0.01 px step. We first quoted a boxcar
        average for how much of a motion each six-second image keeps; that is the response of a rectangular average, not of
        this registration. Measured through the reconstruction’s own masks and registration on simulated images, each image
        does carry a vibration, but at {rImg[0].toFixed(2)} to {Math.round(rImg[1])} times the boxcar value depending on
        frequency, and a pair registers {Math.round(rSharePt[0] * 100)}–{Math.round(rSharePt[1] * 100)}% of the shift between its two
        images for a bright point and {rShareTx[0].toFixed(1)}–{rShareTx[1].toFixed(1)}% for textured ground (Fig. S9). At 2 mm/s the
        pair shifts change by at most {rMaxPt2.toFixed(3)} px for a bright point, about one rounding step, and
        {' '}{rMaxTx2.toFixed(4)} px for texture; in the real image the planted vibrations changed them by
        {' '}{gates.planted_rms_px['0.26'].toFixed(3)} and {gates.planted_rms_px['3.66'].toFixed(3)} px.</p>
        <p>For a simple target moving along the line of sight, the azimuth shift follows its velocity, times
        {' '}{gg.azimuth_m_per_m_s.toFixed(0)} s, and the range shift only its displacement, so its trace is a thin line: the moving
        point passes {rPointWindows} of the first gate’s windows at every frequency tested, and the measurable traces with clutter
        or texture are at most {rWidth.toFixed(2)} as wide as they are long, under the gate’s {gg.axis_ratio_min}. A scene with
        along-track motion, several scatterers or changing reflectivity could draw other shapes; showing that would take its own
        forward simulation. A 3.66 Hz tone is expected to win mode 3 ({(3.66 * gg.window_s).toFixed(2)} cycles a window), so a
        mode alone cannot tell whether a planted motion came back; frequency, place, phase and amplitude together can.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid-2 charts">
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        title="Fig. S8 · Where anything passes, its depth is its mode"
        series={[
          { name: `mode × ${gZc.toFixed(2)} m`, x: gAxis, y: gAxis.map((m) => m * gZc), color: 'var(--muted)', width: 1.4 },
          { name: `its mirror, ${gRep.toFixed(1)} m less`, x: gAxis, y: gAxis.map((m) => gRep - m * gZc), color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '4 3', width: 1.4 },
          ...gDots,
        ]}
        xLabel="the window's winning mode"
        yLabel="best depth, m"
        xTicks={gAxis}
        caption={`Every position that passed every gate in P2-13, in all five cases${gc.desert ? '' : ' (none over open desert)'}, set a little apart by case. ${gates.depth_within_half_cycle} of ${gFeats.length} lie within half a cycle (${(gZc / 2).toFixed(1)} m) of one of the two lines. That is what the depth fit computes, not a discovery: after the gates keep sinusoids, a window whose shifts follow m cycles is steered best by the depth whose phase turns m times across it, or by its mirror. The ${gFeats.length} positions form a handful of clusters, not ${gFeats.length} independent findings.`}
      />
      <LineChart
        directLabels={false}
        logY
        title="Fig. S9 · What the pairs register of a vibration, measured through the reconstruction's own masks and registration"
        series={[
          { name: 'a bright point, 20 mm/s', x: rRows('point', 0.02).map((r) => r.f_hz), y: rRows('point', 0.02).map((r) => r.pair_amp_px.azimuth), color: 'var(--cinnabar)', markers: true },
          { name: 'a bright point, 2 mm/s', x: rRows('point', 0.002).map((r) => r.f_hz), y: rRows('point', 0.002).map((r) => r.pair_amp_px.azimuth), color: 'var(--cinnabar)', dash: '3 3', markers: true },
          { name: 'texture, 20 mm/s', x: rRows('texture', 0.02).map((r) => r.f_hz), y: rRows('texture', 0.02).map((r) => r.pair_amp_px.azimuth), color: 'var(--lapis)', markers: true },
          { name: 'texture, 2 mm/s', x: rRows('texture', 0.002).map((r) => r.f_hz), y: rRows('texture', 0.002).map((r) => r.pair_amp_px.azimuth), color: 'var(--lapis)', dash: '3 3', markers: true },
          { name: 'a boxcar average, 2 mm/s', x: rRows('point', 0.002).map((r) => r.f_hz), y: rRows('point', 0.002).map((r) => r.boxcar_px), color: 'var(--muted)', dash: '1 3', width: 1.4 },
          { name: 'one rounding step', x: [0.1, 10.7], y: [0.01, 0.01], color: 'var(--faint)', width: 1 },
        ]}
        xLabel="frequency of the motion, Hz"
        yLabel="change in the pair shifts, px"
        caption={`Simulated images on the 2022 geometry, the motion put in as a phase history through the Doppler-time mapping (an approximation shared by every motion test here). Amplitudes at the planted frequency of the pair shifts, moved minus still; points below ${(1e-4).toString()} px or exactly zero are not drawn. The boxcar is what a rectangular six-second average would pass; the registration departs from it both ways.`}
      />
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={gates.manifest} />
    <RunDetails manifest={resp.manifest} />
    <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 22px">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">real image · P2-14</span>
        <h3>On its own lines round the base, {oNothing ? 'nothing passes at its own settings' : 'little passes at its own settings'}</h3>
        <table class="data compact">
          <thead><tr><th>positions in a row</th>{oSup.map((P) => <th class="num">{P}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {oRows.map(([label, w, c]) => <tr><td>{label}</td>{oSup.map((P) => <td class="num">{oPos[w][c][P]}</td>)}</tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The reconstruction’s own lines follow Khafre’s base corners, 70.5 m above sea level. Given to the product’s
        geolocation as they were first sent, as heights on the ellipsoid, they sit about 50 px off in range; corrected
        for the geoid, they land on the base (the table). At its own support of four or five neighbouring positions,
        {oNothing ? ' nothing passes on the corrected lines in any image' : ' few positions pass'}; on the uncorrected ones, {oSea.real['4']} pass in the real image and
        {' '}{oSea.twin101['4']} and {oSea.twin102['4']} in the twins. Loosened to a single position, the real image passes
        {' '}{(oW.real['1'] / oTwin1).toFixed(1)} times what the twins do, and open desert in the same image {oDesert['1']} against the
        pyramid’s {oW.real['1']}.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">why · P2-14</span>
        <h3>Its own record: each image holds six seconds of every pixel’s pass</h3>
        <p>For any one pixel, the Doppler frequency of its echo falls steadily as the satellite passes, so each frequency in
        its band was recorded at one moment: the pixel’s own zero-Doppler moment plus the frequency over the Doppler rate.
        The pipeline’s steering makes this conversion itself, with a rate of {oRate.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 })} Hz per
        second at Khafre, so its {Math.round(gates.manifest.params.bank.mask_width_hz).toLocaleString('en-US')} Hz images each hold
        {' '}{oSpan.toFixed(2)} s of every pixel’s pass; it puts the four sides’ zero-Doppler moments within {oSpread.toFixed(2)} s of
        each other. The pairs are frames of a film, each a {oSpan.toFixed(0)}-second exposure, started {Math.round(oFrameMs)} ms apart: a
        shiver repeating several times a second blurs out within every frame.</p>
        <p>Planting the vibrations adds and removes a few positions ({oNew.length} start passing and {oLost} stop, over all
        five supports). In the 0.26 Hz stretch the new ones sit at modes {oNorthModes.join(', ')}, which no 0.26 Hz motion can
        make; in the 3.66 Hz stretch, {oReturned === 1 ? 'one' : oReturned} at the nearest mode. {oDepth.at_mode_depth} of the {oDepth.n} positions passing at a support of one
        have their best depth where their mode puts it, as a frequency fit must.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={own.manifest} />
    </details>
  </section>

  <section id="khufu3d" class="folded">
    <details class="fold">
      <summary><h2>{kHeading}</h2></summary>
    <p class="sub">
      The reconstruction’s tomograms are curtains: along each line, a score at every nominal depth below every position. P2-16
      lays {khufu.manifest.params.lines} lines across Khufu, {khufu.manifest.params.step_m} m apart, running east to west as its own
      east-face lines do, runs the 2022 image through its unchanged code, and stacks the curtains into a volume on a
      {' '}{khufu.manifest.params.step_m} m grid, with the same calculation on a motionless copy of the image beside it. The volume
      holds the method’s fit scores, unsmoothed, and is empty where nothing passed: it is what the method computes, not a
      detection and not a validated depth. <a href={`${base}/underworld/#giza/satellite`}>Open it in the lab</a>, under the
      satellite’s gated reconstruction, where it is drawn beside the surveyed chambers in blue, with its motionless copy and
      open plateau a click away.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">real image · P2-16</span>
        <h3>What passes, and how much of the volume it fills</h3>
        <table class="data compact">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th class="num">positions, support 1</th><th class="num">support 4</th><th class="num">voxels with a score</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>the real image</td><td class="num">{kRes.real.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td class="num">{kRes.real.supported_positions['4']}</td><td class="num">{kR1.voxels_with_score.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a motionless copy</td><td class="num">{kRes.twin101.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td class="num">{kRes.twin101.supported_positions['4']}</td><td class="num">{kT1.voxels_with_score.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>open plateau, the same image (P2-17)</td><td class="num">{kP['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td class="num">{kP['4']}</td><td class="num">{kPV.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Of {(khufu.manifest.params.lines * khufu.manifest.params.positions_per_line).toLocaleString('en-US')} positions, those that pass at
        a support of one hang full depth profiles, so the volume shows columns banded every few metres, the fit’s own cycle,
        wherever a position passed; at the method’s own support of four almost nothing passes. The same raster laid on open
        plateau south-west of Menkaure, where no monument stands, passes nearly as much as the pyramid and far more than the
        copy: the real image’s texture, not the pyramid, sets how much passes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-card">
        <span class="tag measured">a known-chamber check · P2-16</span>
        <h3>Inside the surveyed chambers</h3>
        <p>If the scores located rooms, they would be higher inside the King’s and Queen’s Chambers, the Grand Gallery, the
        passages, the Subterranean Chamber and the ScanPyramids voids than at the same depths elsewhere. In the real image
        they are {kFmt(kR1.mean_score_in_chambers)} on average inside and {kFmt(kR1.mean_score_same_depths_elsewhere)} at the same
        depths elsewhere; in the motionless copy {kFmt(kT1.mean_score_in_chambers)} and {kFmt(kT1.mean_score_same_depths_elsewhere)}.
        {kR1.chamber_voxels_with_score} of the {kR1.chamber_voxels} chamber voxels hold any score in the real image. The chambers are
        small against a 3 m grid and the depth coordinate is nominal, so this is one check among several, stated before it was
        run, not a verdict on its own.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">The lab’s command runs the same raster over any area of the image. Across Khafre, where the claim places
      its shafts, the real image passes {lk.real.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')} positions at a support of one
      and {lk.real.supported_positions['4']} at four, and a motionless copy of the image
      {' '}{lk.twin101.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')} and {lk.twin101.supported_positions['4']}: the pattern of
      Khufu ({kRes.real.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')} and {kRes.twin101.supported_positions['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')}
      at a support of one) and of open plateau ({kP['1'].toLocaleString('en-US')}), where nothing is claimed.
      {' '}<a href={`${base}/underworld/#giza/satellite/radar-lab-khafre-real-p1`}>Open Khafre in the lab</a>.</p>
    <div class="note-card wide">
      <span class="tag measured">real images · P2-21</span>
      <h3>{svHeading}</h3>
      <table class="data compact">
        <thead><tr><th>the same square, run two ways</th><th class="num">positions passing</th><th class="num">passing both</th><th class="num">× chance</th><th class="num">same depth</th><th class="num">by chance</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {svRows.map(([label, r]) => <tr><td>{label}</td><td class="num">{r.passing[0].toLocaleString('en-US')} · {r.passing[1].toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td class="num">{r.both.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="num">{svPct(r.same_depth_share)}</td><td class="num">{svPct(r.same_depth_chance)}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Something under Khafre would not move with the pass that looked at it or with the way the lines were laid; what the
      processing makes of an image would. The lab’s command ran the same square three ways, each beside a motionless copy.
      “Same depth” is the share of positions passing both ways whose best depths lie within half the fit’s cycle, against
      the same pairs shuffled. A real image and its own motionless copy, which keeps only its smoothed brightness and has
      nothing under it, set the scale for agreement that owes nothing to what is below. In the lab, the pass and the
      lines are choices under the gated reconstruction, with the voxels where two runs agree.</p>
    </div>
    <RunDetails manifest={khufu.manifest} />
    <RunDetails manifest={plateau.manifest} />
    <RunDetails manifest={labKhafre.manifest} />
